=== gone_ending ===

{dale_suspicion >= 8 && erasure_level >= 8: -> gone_simultaneous}
{dale_suspicion >= 8: -> gone_dale}
-> gone_erasure


= gone_simultaneous

The staff corridor. Fluorescent lights. The clipboard desk.

Dale is there.

Not the looping version. Not the polo shirt, not the smile, not the cadence of "Big night tonight." This is the other Dale. The one from the spiral steps. Mining jumpsuit, dark fabric, stiff, the stitching from 1973. His hands are empty. He stands at the desk with his weight settled. Fifty years in this spot.

He sees you.

Everyone else's eyes have been sliding past for days. The motel clerk. The guests. The librarian who reported you and then forgot your face. Dale's eyes lock. He tracked you through the records room, the library, the restricted passage, the service corridor, the tunnels. Every door you opened, he noticed. The system noticed through him. High suspicion kept you solid in his perception even as the rest of you thinned out.

He steps forward. Three steps. Deliberate. The fluorescent light buzzes and his shadow falls clean and sharp on the linoleum.

His hand reaches for your shoulder.

It passes through.

His hand enters the space where your shoulder is and continues. His fingers close on the other side. Nothing. No resistance. No surface.

Dale looks at his hand. The expression on his face is not surprise. It's recognition. The problem he tracked has already resolved itself.

Neither of you moves.

Two systems failing in parallel. The suspicion that tracked you and the erasure that thinned you, both reaching their endpoint at the same moment, in the same corridor, under the same fluorescent lights. Dale's hand passes through you because there isn't enough of you left to grip. You don't feel his hand because there isn't enough of you left to feel it.

The fluorescent light buzzes. The hum in the floor is louder than the light.

The space where you were standing smooths over. Not instantly. Gradually. The air fills in. The outline softens. The corridor returns to what it was before you walked into it.

Dale's hand drops to his side. He turns back to the desk. Picks up the clipboard. The smile forms. The muscles engage, hold, release.

"Big night tonight. Full house. Make it real, people."

He says it to an empty corridor. He's been saying it to an empty corridor for a long time.

-> gone_shared


= gone_dale

The staff corridor. Fluorescent lights. The clipboard desk.

Dale is there.

Not the polo shirt. Not the khakis. Not the clipboard version who checked you in every night, who smiled and said "make it real" and sent you to your zone. That Dale was a performance.

This Dale is wearing a mining jumpsuit. Dark fabric, stiff, the stitching from another decade. 1973. The same jumpsuit from the spiral steps. His hands are empty. No clipboard. No headset. No props. He stands at the check-in desk. Part of it. Built into the frame.

He was waiting.

{dale_suspicion >= 9: He was thorough. The system was thorough. You found the employee records. The restricted passage. The library archives. The service corridor. The deep rooms. Every layer of this place that wasn't supposed to be seen, you pulled back. Every question that wasn't supposed to be asked, you asked. Dale tracked all of it. One point at a time, invisible in isolation, until the total crossed a line that couldn't be uncrossed.}

{dale_suspicion == 8: One threshold too many. Not a campaign of investigation. Not a systematic dismantling. Just one moment, one question, one door that tipped the balance. The system has a number and you reached it.}

He steps forward. One step. Two. Three.

Not fast. Not aggressive. Administrative. His face is blank. Not angry. Not satisfied. Operational.

His grip closes on your arm. The hand is warm. The fingers are strong in a way that has nothing to do with muscle. Fifty years of management in five fingers on your bicep.

The corridor changes.

Not dramatically. The fluorescent lights don't flicker. The walls don't shift. But the space behind you, the space that was the staff entrance, the parking lot, the road, the desert, the world outside Veinrock. That space is no longer there. The corridor ends where it ends. Behind you is wall. The door you walked through doesn't exist.

Dale's grip doesn't loosen. He walks you forward. Your feet move because the floor moves under them. Three steps. Five. The corridor narrows. The fluorescent lights thin out. The hum rises. The mine is behind the walls, underneath the floor, above the ceiling. The mine has always been behind everything.

The grip releases.

Dale turns. Walks back to the desk. Sits down. Picks up the clipboard. The smile forms.

"Big night tonight. Full house. Make it real, people."

The corridor behind him is empty. It was always empty. Nobody walked through it tonight. Nobody has walked through it in a long time.

The loop resets.

-> gone_shared


= gone_erasure

Nobody is at the check-in desk.

The clipboard is there. The sign-in sheet is there. The pen on a string is there. But the desk is empty and the corridor is empty and the fluorescent lights hum over nothing.

You walk past. Through the staff entrance. Down the corridor. Past the costume room, past the break room, past the locker where your dead miner jumpsuit hangs on a hook. The building is running. You can hear it: the bass speakers in the upper zones, the fog machines cycling, the screaming from the guest corridors above. Full house. The biggest crowd of the week. The machine is at capacity.

Nobody sees you walk through.

You walk to Zone 2 on muscle memory. Your feet know the route. The corridor. The turn. The narrow passage with the timber supports and the foam rubble and the fiber-optic fissures in the ceiling. Your zone. Your position. Four nights in this hole, hitting this window, producing this startle.

You get in the boo hole.

The glow tape mark is on the floor. The pneumatic latch is cold under your hand. The slit in the panel shows the corridor outside, lit amber and foggy. The next group is coming. You can hear them. Four people, maybe five, shuffling through the narrow passage, bunched together, hands on each other's shoulders. Voices tight.

You wait. The first shadow crosses the glow tape mark.

You hit the window.

The bang is perfect. The pneumatic hinge fires, the panel slams open, and you are there, framed in the gap, exactly right. The timing. The angle. The scream that rips from your chest, short and sharp and real. Four nights of practice distilled into one clean execution.

The group doesn't react.

They shuffle forward. Someone laughs at something someone else said. A voice: "This part's not that scary." They walk past the open window, past the gap where you are standing, past the scream you just produced, and nobody flinches. Nobody startles. Nobody's eyes land on the window.

Their eyes slide past.

The window bangs shut. The latch clicks. You stand in the boo hole and the group rounds the corner and disappears and the corridor is empty.

You step out.

The corridor. The amber light. The fog pooling at your ankles. Another group is coming. You can hear them, further back, moving through Zone 1. In three minutes they'll be here.

You stand in the corridor. One foot away from the boo hole, in the space where the actors stand between groups, where Bex used to do the stalker walk, where the guests are supposed to see you and scream. You stand there.

The group arrives.

Their eyes slide past.

A woman walks within arm's reach of you. Her gaze passes through the space where you are and settles on the corridor ahead. A man behind her adjusts his grip on his friend's shoulder. He's looking at the walls, at the ceiling, at the timber supports. Not at you. Not because he's avoiding you. Because the space you occupy doesn't register as containing anything.

They pass. The corridor empties.

You're standing in the mine. In the show. In the machine. And the machine doesn't know you're here.

The mine didn't do anything.

It didn't reach out. It didn't grip you. It didn't close a corridor behind you or send a manager in a jumpsuit to walk you into the dark. It just waited. Mornings of the motel clerk forgetting your face. Afternoons of the world getting thinner around you. The doorway. The stairwell. The deep rooms. Every threshold you crossed took a piece, and the pieces accumulated, and the accumulation was patient, and patience is the same as certainty if you wait long enough.

Thin enough is the same as not there.

-> gone_shared


= gone_portal

The amber light doesn't feel like light.

It fills you. Not your eyes. Your body. Your bones. The hum doesn't feel like sound. It enters through your feet and rises through your skeleton and settles in the fluid behind your eyes and stays there.

You stepped off the edge. You chose this. No ritual knowledge, no containment circuit, no map of the other side, no guide, no protection. Just the edge and the step and the nothing below.

The nothing receives you.

Not falling. Falling has direction. Falling has a bottom. This is absorption. The amber light closes over you. The heat is total. The pressure is total. Every surface of your body meets resistance. Not from air. Not from stone. From the absence itself, pushing back against the shape of you, testing the edges, finding where you end.

Where you end is getting closer to where you begin.

The Figure watched. From the far side of the circle, through the burlap mask, it watched you step forward. It didn't stop you. It didn't reach out. It didn't follow. Whatever the Figure is, Mara or what's left of Mara or the memory of Mara pressed into the shape of the mine, it stood on the rim and watched and did not move.

The portal breathes.

You feel the exhale from inside it now. Not as pressure pushing outward. As expansion. The nothing gets larger. The edges of the shaft, the stone rim, the carvings. They recede. The amber light brightens, whitens, loses color. The hum changes pitch, drops below hearing, becomes a vibration in the structure of things. In the atoms. In the space between atoms.

The other side takes you. Not violently. Not gradually. Completely. Without remainder. You pass into it and there is no boundary, no threshold, no moment of crossing. One state becomes another.

You are in the mine.

The mine is in you.

The distinction was always temporary.

-> gone_shared


= gone_shared

{priority == "find_mara":
    You came to find her.
    Mara Castillo. Day 10. She went into the mine and didn't come out. The Order lost contact. Silas made the call. You drove through the desert to a town that barely existed on the map and you took a job screaming in a hole and you followed a figure in a burlap mask deeper every night because someone was missing and finding them was the mission.
    You followed her trail all the way down. You just couldn't follow it out.
}

{priority == "investigate_paranormal":
    You came to understand it.
    The portal. The phenomenon underneath the mine underneath the town. The Order's 1971 report, filed and forgotten. Mara's recordings. The containment system that predated every structure built on this land. You came to document the threat, to assess its parameters, to determine whether the thing erasing Veinrock could be stopped.
    You got close enough to see it. You just couldn't get far enough away to report it.
}

{mara_clues >= 3:
    You found her trail. The notebook page in the restricted passage. The recorder in Gloria's drawer. The handprint in the deep tunnels. The pages, the map, the taxonomy. You traced her from the surface to the spiral steps, from the first day to the tenth, from the agent she was to the figure she became. You got close. Closer than anyone since she went through.
    It wasn't enough.
}

{mara_clues >= 1 && mara_clues < 3:
    You found fragments. A name on an incorporation document. A voice on a recorder. Pieces of a trail that someone left in a place designed to erase trails. They almost added up.
    Almost.
}

{mara_clues == 0: You found almost nothing. A town. A mine. A job. A hum in the floor that got louder every night. You walked through five days of a place that was dissolving around you and you didn't collect enough pieces to understand what was happening or who it had happened to before you.}

{bex_relationship >= 5:
    Bex said stay left. At the motel, in the daylight, squinting against the sun. Decades of tracing containment lines in the dark, fading a little more each season, holding the circuit shut one carved groove at a time. They gave you everything they had left. The ritual. The route. The warning about tonight.
    You tried. That's the thing Bex would want to know. You tried.
}

{has_mara_recording: Day eight. Mara's voice on the recorder, steady and fraying. The hum in the background. "The fear is not a byproduct. It's the product." She understood it before you did. She documented the mechanism while it was consuming her. Professional to the end. You listened to her voice in Gloria's diner and you knew what this place was and you came back anyway.}

The portal reaches capacity tonight.

Two hundred people in the queue. The biggest crowd of the week. Families. Couples. Teenagers in packs, nervous and loud. Every one of them walking through the mine entrance, past the fog and the speakers and the recorded warning, into The Hollows. Every one of them screaming. The real screams underneath the theatrical ones, the frequency that comes from the body, from the autonomic system, from the part of the brain that knows before the conscious mind does.

The portal breathes faster. The exhale cycles compress. Three seconds, two, one. The pause between breaths narrows to nothing. The hum drops below hearing and becomes a vibration in the stone, in the building, in the ground underneath the parking lot, in the road, in the desert.

The portal opens all the way.

Not gradually. Not the way it's been opening for fifty years, fed and widened one carved glyph at a time. All at once. The containment fails. The carvings on the rim, the ones Bex traced and retraced for decades, the ones Martin Aldrich checked every morning before his shift. They crack. All of them. The circuit breaks and the lock opens and the thing underneath the mine underneath the town reaches the surface.

Veinrock disappears.

Not violently. Not with fire or sound or any of the things that should accompany a town ceasing to exist. Quietly. The mine goes first. Then the building on top of it. Then the parking lot. Then the road. Then the diner, the motel, the library, the gravel lots, the houses with their porches and their desert lawns. Then Gloria behind the counter. Then Tuck in Zone 3, hand on the fake rubble wall, mid-scare. Then the guests, two hundred of them, screaming in corridors that are no longer there.

The desert smooths over.

Rock and sand and sagebrush, unbroken, the way it was before the mine and before the town and before anyone carved containment symbols into stone to hold shut something that was never meant to be opened.

A blank spot on the map. A place that GPS returns no results for. A name that doesn't appear in any census, any post office database, any satellite image. Veinrock, Nevada. Population zero. Population nothing. Population: the question doesn't apply because the place the question refers to does not exist.

You are in the blank spot.

Not underneath it. Not beside it. In it. In the space where the town was, where the mine was, where the portal breathed and the building fed it and the guests screamed and the actors screamed back and the hum was in everything. You are in the nothing that replaced it.

You were here.

Past tense. Not because you left. Because the here is gone too.

-> DONE
